The '''Zong script''' was adopted for the writing of [[Hachina language|Hachina]] by decree of the king in the sixteenth century, displacing [[Dalewa]], the traditional script, from official use. The script was not devised for Hachinawa and does not fit it, and writing Hachina in it required a system of transcription in four parts. That system, the characters coined for it, and the consequences of the adoption are treated here. An overview of both scripts is given in [[Scripts of Hachinawa]].

The adoption was a policy of the court rather than a gradual influence, and it was understood as such by those it displaced. It is treated as an event of the reign in [[History of Hachinawa (1538-1606)|the history of the period]].

== Origins ==

The system was developed over several decades, and its origin precedes the decree that made it official.

The court had been training men in Zong from the 1520s, when the crown and the great houses engaged merchants from the Zong colonies to teach the language to Hachinawa students. The men trained in that programme were the only Hachina able to read Zong, and they were the first to write Hachina in Zong characters, in order to place Hachina names in documents that Zong officials would accept.

Until the middle of the sixteenth century the practice was confined to proper nouns. Names were written with characters borrowed for their sound and not their sense, following the practice of the Zong themselves, and the result was an approximation legible to a Zong reader and not to a Hachina one. Names were written in this way in the documents of the [[Treaty of Anping]], in the correspondence of the 1530s, and in the records of the colonial officials.

The decree extended the requirement from proper nouns to the whole language. The adoption of the Zong script for official use applied to enactments, registers and correspondence, and the method that served for names did not serve for these. The court accordingly charged the scribes with devising a system, and the work is attributed to a commission of the corps of Zong-speakers and of the temple scribes.

The commission is credited to Daleha - from ''dale'', "speech", a personal name formed as that of [[Nachiha]] is, from a root with the name-forming element. Daleha was a scribe of the court and of the temple establishments, trained in ''Dalewa'' as the temple taught it and afterwards in Zong by the merchants of the programme, and he is the first Hachina recorded as competent in both scripts and both languages. His principal collaborator was Tsiha - from ''tsin'', "sign", "character" - a temple scribe trained in the older tradition, and the design of the system is credited to the two together.

A Zong name is attached to the commission as well. The teacher engaged in the 1520s whose instruction is recorded as having continued longest at the court is named in the Hachinawa records as Tân Kéng-bûn (陳景文), a merchant of the Zong colony who remained at the Hachinawa court after his engagement ended. He is credited in the Hachinawa account with the practice of borrowing characters for sound, which he taught as the ordinary Zong method of writing a foreign name. The phonetic principle reached the Hachina through him; the semantic and classifier elements were added by the commission.

The record dates the commission's work to the years after the decree without giving it a single year. It produced a system of four parts.

== Semantic use ==
The common words are written with the Zong character whose meaning corresponds, and are read as the Hachina word. The character for water is read ''ku''; the character for king is read ''hik''; the character for centre is read ''hachin''.

This is the largest part of the system and the least troublesome, because the meaning of the character is the same whoever reads it.

A Zong reader recovers the substance of a Hachina document - the persons, places, things and actions it is about - because the characters for those are shared. Beyond them he recovers little. Everything that is not a character of that kind is closed to him. The grammar of Hachinawa is not written in shared characters: the particles, the verbal suffixes and the inflections that make a Hachina sentence a sentence have no Zong equivalents and are written with coined or phonetic characters that carry no meaning to a Zong reader at all.

A Hachina document is therefore partly legible and not intelligible. A Zong official reading one could establish what it concerned - grain, gold, a certain city, a certain king, a payment - and could not determine what was asserted about them. He would know the document was about grain and not whether the grain had been received or demanded.

== Characters coined for Hachina ==
Hachina words with no Zong equivalent are written with characters composed for the purpose from Zong elements. The Hachina character for ''Dalewa'' itself is composed of the elements for speech and for realm, in that order, following the construction of the name.

A character coined in this way is read only as Hachina, and carries no Zong reading. The Zong cannot read them, and the coinage is therefore the part of a document that a Zong reader cannot penetrate.

== Zong symbol elements ==
The coined Hachina characters are built from Zong elements rather than invented from nothing, and the elements used are the Zong radicals: the small set of semantic components from which Zong characters are themselves composed.

A Hachina character is formed by selecting the Zong radical that corresponds to the general category of the word and combining it with a second element that distinguishes the word within that category. The Hachina character for a river takes the Zong radical for water and adds a distinguishing element; the character for a granary takes the radical for grain. A reader of Zong meeting the character for the first time can infer its category from the radical even where he cannot read it, and a Hachina reader is using the same radical to find the character in a list.

The practice was extended further than Zong practice goes in one respect. The Hahkina kept their own classifiers alongside the Zong radicals, writing the Hachina mark beneath the character where a Zong writer would have relied on the radical alone. The result is a character that carries two classifications at once - one legible to the Zong, one to the Hahkina - and a page of official Hachina is dense with marks that only one of the two readerships can see.

== Phonetic use ==
Names are written phonetically, with Zong characters borrowed for their sound rather than their meaning. This is the practice already established for Hachina names in Zong documents: the people's own name is written with three characters read for their sound and not for their sense.

The method is inexact. Zong has no nasalised vowels and no glottal distinctions, so a Hachina name written with Zong characters is approximate, and the same name appears written differently by different scribes. Names are the least stable part of the written record of the period, and the forms in which Hachina names reached Zong documents are Zong approximations rather than transcriptions.

== Classifiers retained ==
The Hachina classifiers are written alongside the Zong characters, so that a character with Zong meanings is marked for the sense intended in Hachinawa. A Zong character standing for "people" may be marked with the Hachina classifier for folk, and the mark tells a Hachina reader which word it stands for.

The mark is invisible to a Zong reader, who reads the character and not the classifier.

== Numerals and the calendar ==
The numerals of ''Dalewa'' were displaced along with the characters. Official documents of the period carry dates and quantities in the Zong numerals, and a Hachina writer of the court used them in preference to his own.

The ''Dalewa'' numerals were not lost. They continued in the temple records, in the calendar as the priests kept it, and in the writing of the traditionalists, and a granary at a temple in the seventeenth century is recorded in the old numerals. The distinction between the two sets became one of the marks separating official from temple writing, in the same way as the scripts themselves.

The calendar notation fared worse, because it was a system rather than a set of signs. The Hachina dated by the year of the reigning king, and the Zong dated by their own continuous reckoning. A document of the period may carry either or both, and the coincidence of the two systems in a single record is the ordinary means by which the reigns of the kings have been fixed.

