== Two readings ==

The table above shows the point on which the whole system turns. A character written in a Hachina document has two readings and they are unrelated. 水 is read ''chúi'' by a Zong and ''ku'' by a Hachina; 王 is ''ông'' in Zong and ''hik'' in Hachinawa; 金 is ''kim'' and ''sho''.

And the readings are single. [[Zong language|Zong]] is a tonal language, and its characters carry more than one reading: the same character is read differently according to the tone it takes and according to whether the reading is the literary or the colloquial one. Hachina has no tones, so a character written in a Hachina document has one reading and no other, and the multiplicity that a Zong reader must keep in mind does not exist for a Hachina one.

This is the one respect in which the Hachina system is simpler than the Zong system it borrowed. The characters were taken over with their meanings, which are shared; they were not taken over with their tones, which are not; and what remains is a set of signs each of which stands for exactly one Hachina word.

The character is a symbol of the meaning and not of the sound. Semantic characters are read aloud in whichever language the reader speaks, and the same page produces two entirely different recitations depending on who reads it. A Zong official reading a Hachina document aloud is understood by nobody in the room; a Hachina reading a Zong document aloud is understood by nobody either.

The effect is that official Hachina of this period is legible to both peoples and pronounceable by only one at a time. The Zong could read the sense of a Hachina enactment without being able to say it, and the Hachina could read a Zong despatch without knowing how it sounded. The system worked because the object was comprehension and not speech, and it was adopted for that reason.

The exception is the phonetic characters. A character borrowed for its sound is read as the Zong word, and a Hachina name written phonetically is pronounced as the Zong approximation of it. Hence the forms in which Hachina names reached Zong documents: ''Hahkina'' for a people who called themselves Hachina, and the three characters of a king's name read as the nearest Zong sounds to a Hachina word that Zong cannot say.

So a Hachina document contains two kinds of writing at once: characters read as Hachina words, which a Zong can understand and cannot pronounce, and characters read as Zong sounds, which a Zong can pronounce and which are not Hachina at all.

== Phonetic rendering ==

A Hachina word can also be written phonetically, with Zong characters borrowed for their sound. The same word therefore looks entirely different in the two renderings, and a Hachina document may contain both.

