|-
! Hachina word !! Meaning !! Semantic rendering !! Phonetic rendering
|-
| ku || water || 水 || 库
|-
| hik || king || 王 || 希克
|-
| kuhik || water-king, a title || 水王 || 库希克
|-
| dalewa || Dalewa || 言邦 || 拉勒瓦
|-
| hiknawe || the king's assembly || 王議 || 希克那衛
|-
| hacha || grain || 粟 || 哈其
|-
| sho || gold || 金 || 沙何
|}

Ordinary words are written semantically in Hachinawa documents and phonetically only where a Zong must pronounce them. Names are written phonetically in both scripts, because a name has no meaning to render: ''Tsulhachi'' and ''Nachiha'' are phonetic even in a Hachina document.

== Losses ==

The phonetic rendering is approximate, and the approximations are systematic. Zong has no nasalised vowels, so the Hachina nasals are written as plain vowels or as a final ''-n''. Zong has no glottal stop in the positions Hachina uses it, and it is lost. Hachina has no tones and Zong does, so the tones of a Zong reading are absent from the Hachina word a character stands for, and a Hachina speaker reading the borrowed characters aloud gives them without the tone the character carries in Zong. Hachina ''r'', ''sh'' and ''f'' have no Zong counterparts and are written ''l'', ''h'' and ''h''. And final consonants become full syllables, so a Hachina word of two syllables may reach a Zong document as four characters.

The result is that a Hachina name in a Zong document is longer, flatter and nasal-free than the name itself. ''Hahkina'' is the Zong rendering of Hachinawa - 哈其那 for a people whose own name is written 中民.

== Why the corps remained necessary ==

The partial legibility of the system did not remove the need for Hachina officials competent in Zong. It explains the need.

A Hachina document could be laid before a Zong official and the subject of it established. It could not be acted upon without a man who could read the grammar. The business of the colony was conducted with interpreters throughout the period, and the men of the corps that the court had trained since the 1520s were employed as much in reading Hachina documents to Zong officials as in reading Zong documents to the court.

In the other direction the position was worse. A Zong document laid before a Hachina reader was legible in the same partial way, and a Hachina reader without Zong could establish its subject and not its terms. This is the practical reason the court paid for the teaching of Zong, and the reason the men it produced were valuable: the written system they had devised made documents exchangeable without making them comprehensible.

== System in use ==
A Hachina document of the sixteenth century is therefore legible at three levels. A Zong reader recovers the subject, because the nouns and verbs are in characters whose meanings are shared, and recovers nothing else. A Hachina reader recovers the Hachina, because he knows the readings and the classifiers. And neither recovers the other's names, since a Hachina reader cannot pronounce a Zong approximation of a Hachina name back into the name itself, and a Zong reader cannot read a coined character at all.

The consequence for the record is that the written Hachina of this period is thinner than the spoken language. The characters carry the sense of a document and not its sound, and the sound of the language in the sixteenth century is known only where a Hachina writer set out a reading in ''Dalewa'' beside the Zong characters, which the scribes of the court did for the vocabulary of administration and not for ordinary speech.

The Zong script was adopted for the writing of Hachinawa in the sixteenth century by decree of the king, in a policy the court understood as competition with the power it dealt with.

The reasoning was that a state able to read the Zong papers, correspond with the colonial administration in its own characters, and keep its own records in the same form as its creditors would deal with the Zong on better terms. The adoption was not gradual and was not a matter of convenience in translation: the [[History of Hachinawa (1538-1606)|constitutional framework]] of [[Nachiha]] was composed in the Zong script by the intention of the court that drafted it, and the instrument that fixed the Hachinawa constitution therefore also displaced the characters in which the Hahkina had written for eight centuries.

== Consequences ==

The decree transferred to the crown and its scribes a competence that had belonged to the temples. ''Dalewa'' was taught in the temple schools and kept by the priestly lineages; the new script was learned by the officials of the capital and the mercantile houses of the coast, and the schools in which it was taught were the crown's.

The displacement is the origin of the period's political division. The priestly lineages lost the standing that came from keeping the only script in which the state wrote; the lineages of the plain lost it with them; and the court and the coast acquired it.

== Traditionalist use ==

''Dalewa'' did not cease to be used. It survived in the temples, in the records of the priesthood and in the writing of those who opposed the policy, and the surviving documents of the traditionalist movement of the sixteenth century are written in it.

The movement's argument, recorded in the script it was defending, was that a state which wrote in another people's characters had ceased to be the centre of anything.

